1
00:00:01,330 --> 00:00:05,970
Hello, and welcome to the third week of this MOC,

2
00:00:05,970 --> 00:00:08,330
Introduction to Biorisk Management.

3
00:00:08,330 --> 00:00:10,330
In this lecture, I will discuss

4
00:00:10,330 --> 00:00:21,440
the concept of engineering controls.

5
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:23,640
As an introduction, in this lecture,

6
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:28,240
I will introduce you to the basics of engineering controls.

7
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:31,060
Now, I understand that you may be coming

8
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:34,480
from different backgrounds, and generally as biologists,

9
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,160
and I'm a biologist too,

10
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:39,360
we do not know much about engineering.

11
00:00:39,360 --> 00:00:42,640
However, as a Biorisk Manager or a Biosafety Officer,

12
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:46,440
we must look deeper into engineering,

13
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:48,840
and try and enhance our knowledge

14
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:51,000
as we progress in our career.

15
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:54,560
One of the equipment which I will be focusing on

16
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:59,200
in this lecture module is the Biological Safety Cabinet.

17
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:04,000
I have prepared some videos for you in my own laboratory,

18
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,040
in which I have demonstrated

19
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:10,900
as to how you should operate the Biological Safety Cabinet.

20
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:17,090
Let us look into this lecture in detail.

21
00:01:17,090 --> 00:01:20,730
These are the learning objectives of this particular lecture.

22
00:01:20,730 --> 00:01:23,370
Now, this is a new term which I am using here,

23
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:26,170
and the term is Facility Design.

24
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:29,650
Now, Facility Design is an engineering term,

25
00:01:29,650 --> 00:01:31,570
and as a Biorisk Manager,

26
00:01:31,570 --> 00:01:35,450
you will have to communicate with engineers and architects

27
00:01:35,450 --> 00:01:38,130
when you intend to design your facility,

28
00:01:38,130 --> 00:01:41,150
or even when you are operating the facility.

29
00:01:41,150 --> 00:01:44,690
And this Facility Design is one of the components

30
00:01:44,690 --> 00:01:47,050
which I've included in this MOOC,

31
00:01:47,050 --> 00:01:49,250
so that you are cognizant or aware

32
00:01:49,250 --> 00:01:52,830
of the basic design of a facility.

33
00:01:52,830 --> 00:01:54,090
The second learning objective

34
00:01:54,090 --> 00:01:56,970
will be the Biological Safety Cabinet itself,

35
00:01:56,970 --> 00:02:00,090
and its operation, and I would also like you

36
00:02:00,090 --> 00:02:03,970
to look at interdependencies between different controls.

37
00:02:03,970 --> 00:02:07,090
Now, although we have five controls,

38
00:02:07,090 --> 00:02:09,970
which are elimination, substitution,

39
00:02:10,870 --> 00:02:14,290
engineering controls, administrative controls,

40
00:02:14,290 --> 00:02:16,450
and personal protective equipment,

41
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:19,210
these controls are not used individually,

42
00:02:19,210 --> 00:02:21,050
rather they are used concurrently.

43
00:02:21,050 --> 00:02:24,310
For instance, if you need to use an engineering control,

44
00:02:24,310 --> 00:02:26,530
which is a biological safety cabinet,

45
00:02:26,530 --> 00:02:27,570
you still have to refer

46
00:02:27,570 --> 00:02:30,130
to the standard operating procedure,

47
00:02:30,130 --> 00:02:33,010
which is an administrative control.

48
00:02:33,010 --> 00:02:38,010
And this brings you into the area of interdependencies

49
00:02:39,650 --> 00:02:41,970
between different controls.

50
00:02:41,970 --> 00:02:44,050
Let us look into this in more detail.

51
00:02:45,090 --> 00:02:46,490
These are your learning outcomes.

52
00:02:46,490 --> 00:02:49,010
So the section on learning outcomes,

53
00:02:49,010 --> 00:02:50,770
as you must be aware by now,

54
00:02:50,770 --> 00:02:54,170
is what your quiz assesses.

55
00:02:54,170 --> 00:02:57,330
The quiz has been designed to assess your learning outcomes.

56
00:02:57,330 --> 00:02:59,690
And you should understand the basic elements

57
00:02:59,690 --> 00:03:01,410
of facility design,

58
00:03:01,410 --> 00:03:03,690
which we will go into step by step,

59
00:03:03,690 --> 00:03:06,490
and we'll learn how to design a facility,

60
00:03:06,490 --> 00:03:08,850
just the basic layout, a planned layout,

61
00:03:08,850 --> 00:03:10,510
and understand the operation

62
00:03:10,510 --> 00:03:13,010
of the biological safety cabinet.

63
00:03:13,010 --> 00:03:20,980
This is what we focus on for this current lecture.

64
00:03:20,980 --> 00:03:22,600
Now, this is a containment facility.

65
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:27,480
This is an actual photograph from my laboratory.

66
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:28,400
And as you can see,

67
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:30,620
there is a lot of engineering controls over here.

68
00:03:31,340 --> 00:03:32,540
These are actually the switches

69
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:35,460
which control different motors and they are circuit breakers.

70
00:03:35,460 --> 00:03:38,180
But what I would like to draw your attention to

71
00:03:38,180 --> 00:03:40,060
is this particular unit.

72
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:41,900
Now, this facility flow,

73
00:03:41,900 --> 00:03:44,640
which you see is actually above the laboratory.

74
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:46,740
And this is the HEPA filter unit.

75
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:48,060
So the air from the laboratory

76
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:49,940
is pumped through this filter.

77
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:51,260
And in a facility,

78
00:03:51,260 --> 00:03:56,260
there is no instrumentation or engineering mechanism

79
00:03:56,380 --> 00:03:57,700
inside the facility itself.

80
00:03:57,700 --> 00:04:00,200
Everything is in a flow or service flow,

81
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:02,840
which is located above the facility.

82
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:09,060
Let us look into this in detail.

83
00:04:09,060 --> 00:04:10,740
Okay, now the first concept

84
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:12,900
is the concept of biosafety level.

85
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:15,060
So we have four biosafety levels,

86
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:18,700
as I have discussed in the WHO laboratory

87
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:22,260
biosafety manual lecture.

88
00:04:22,260 --> 00:04:27,260
And these are actually based on the levels one to four.

89
00:04:31,210 --> 00:04:35,190
So this is a basic description

90
00:04:35,190 --> 00:04:40,190
for each BSL level of BSL1, BSL2, BSL3 and BSL4.

91
00:04:42,270 --> 00:04:44,790
Now this particular table has been derived

92
00:04:44,790 --> 00:04:48,510
from the WHO laboratory biosafety manual third edition.

93
00:04:48,510 --> 00:04:51,150
Now let us take only one feature

94
00:04:52,430 --> 00:04:54,610
which is the isolation of the laboratory.

95
00:04:54,610 --> 00:04:57,070
So isolation of laboratory is one feature.

96
00:04:57,070 --> 00:05:01,290
So no, no, no for BSL1 and two.

97
00:05:01,290 --> 00:05:02,710
However, in BSL3,

98
00:05:02,710 --> 00:05:07,710
you have to have a isolated laboratory as well as four.

99
00:05:08,870 --> 00:05:12,030
Now what it means, an isolated laboratory means

100
00:05:12,030 --> 00:05:16,150
you should have a defined space for an activity.

101
00:05:16,150 --> 00:05:18,550
So when you're working with risk group two and above,

102
00:05:18,550 --> 00:05:20,190
you must have a defined space.

103
00:05:20,190 --> 00:05:23,030
And this space is a containment facility.

104
00:05:23,030 --> 00:05:25,670
This is what is stated here

105
00:05:25,670 --> 00:05:28,190
in terms of isolation of laboratory.

106
00:05:28,190 --> 00:05:31,310
Room sealable for decontamination.

107
00:05:31,310 --> 00:05:32,810
Now, you may be asking me

108
00:05:32,810 --> 00:05:35,350
why you need to seal a room for decontamination.

109
00:05:35,350 --> 00:05:39,430
This is because the decontamination is done using ozone

110
00:05:39,430 --> 00:05:40,790
and the room must be sealed.

111
00:05:40,790 --> 00:05:44,430
So in the case of BSL1 and two, it is no, no.

112
00:05:44,430 --> 00:05:47,970
However, in three and four, we need to decontaminate the room.

113
00:05:47,970 --> 00:05:49,670
So the room is basically sealed off.

114
00:05:49,670 --> 00:05:50,690
The doors are sealed off.

115
00:05:50,690 --> 00:05:52,230
All the apertures are sealed off.

116
00:05:52,230 --> 00:05:53,470
The ventilation is sealed.

117
00:05:53,470 --> 00:05:56,190
And then ozone gas is pumped into the room

118
00:05:56,190 --> 00:05:59,450
and this room is sterilized or disinfected.

119
00:06:00,450 --> 00:06:03,130
And this is why the room must be sealed.

120
00:06:03,130 --> 00:06:06,330
And these are various parameters.

121
00:06:06,330 --> 00:06:11,330
So the criteria in which the WHO has specified,

122
00:06:14,670 --> 00:06:17,630
for instance, biological safety cabinet is desirable

123
00:06:17,630 --> 00:06:21,870
in BSL2, but in the level three and level four,

124
00:06:21,870 --> 00:06:25,270
it is a yes and a yes.

125
00:06:25,270 --> 00:06:28,630
So this is a guideline which you can refer to

126
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:31,270
when developing your own specific facilities

127
00:06:31,430 --> 00:06:35,070
or upgrading for example, from BSL1 to BSL2

128
00:06:35,070 --> 00:06:38,150
or BSL2 to BSL3 and so on and so forth.

129
00:06:38,150 --> 00:06:40,790
Now, please take note that BSL4 laboratories

130
00:06:40,790 --> 00:06:44,670
are very expensive to build, operate and maintain.

131
00:06:44,670 --> 00:06:48,510
And these are generally specialized and located only maybe

132
00:06:48,510 --> 00:06:53,510
at one centralized facility at the national level.

133
00:06:53,670 --> 00:06:56,870
So BSL4 is generally not discussed

134
00:06:56,870 --> 00:07:01,350
in lectures related to BSL

135
00:07:01,350 --> 00:07:05,110
as we are basically focusing on the one, two and three.

136
00:07:06,230 --> 00:07:07,670
This is what we can work with

137
00:07:07,670 --> 00:07:15,050
and this is what we can afford to work with.

138
00:07:15,050 --> 00:07:17,170
Okay, let us look at the containment facility.

139
00:07:17,170 --> 00:07:18,730
Now, this is a sectional model.

140
00:07:18,730 --> 00:07:20,050
I have sectioned the facility

141
00:07:20,050 --> 00:07:22,770
to show you how it's actually planned.

142
00:07:22,770 --> 00:07:24,890
So as you can see, the most important thing

143
00:07:24,890 --> 00:07:28,170
is this biohazard symbol, which sits on the door

144
00:07:28,170 --> 00:07:29,870
when you enter this laboratory at the entry

145
00:07:30,350 --> 00:07:31,950
and the exit point.

146
00:07:31,950 --> 00:07:34,470
And then you have a sealed facility,

147
00:07:34,470 --> 00:07:38,630
a sealed what is WHO refers to as a facility

148
00:07:38,630 --> 00:07:41,190
which can be sealed for decontamination.

149
00:07:42,390 --> 00:07:44,030
Then you have directional airflow.

150
00:07:44,030 --> 00:07:47,470
So the air should be directed into the facility

151
00:07:47,470 --> 00:07:51,880
of course, via the HEPA filter.

152
00:07:51,880 --> 00:07:56,160
Then we have HEPA filtered air which exits the facility.

153
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:59,500
So the rate of flow of air is maintained

154
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:02,780
so that this entire facility is at negative pressure

155
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:06,520
with reference to the external atmospheric pressure.

156
00:08:07,980 --> 00:08:11,860
Then you have your personnel entering through an airlock.

157
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:16,980
You have a safety shower when you exit the facility

158
00:08:16,980 --> 00:08:19,180
and then you have an effluent treatment system

159
00:08:19,180 --> 00:08:22,180
which is located in the service flow below the facility.

160
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:26,580
You have an autoclave biological safety cabinet.

161
00:08:26,580 --> 00:08:29,060
So this is the basic setup of the facility.

162
00:08:29,060 --> 00:08:31,800
So you begin with a laboratory

163
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:36,220
which is this blue section over here

164
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:40,360
and then it's sealed so it can be decontaminated.

165
00:08:40,360 --> 00:08:44,520
You have airlocks for entry, shower out

166
00:08:44,520 --> 00:08:46,720
when you exit the facility.

167
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:48,400
You have an autoclave which is located

168
00:08:48,400 --> 00:08:51,980
in the facility itself, a biological safety cabinet.

169
00:08:51,980 --> 00:08:55,680
And of course, you have negative air pressure

170
00:08:55,680 --> 00:08:57,800
with HEPA filters in and out.

171
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:01,160
And this is the effluent treatment system

172
00:09:01,160 --> 00:09:03,980
which manages all the liquid waste

173
00:09:03,980 --> 00:09:09,870
which is produced by this facility.

174
00:09:09,870 --> 00:09:13,510
So BSL-1 laboratories are basic laboratories

175
00:09:13,510 --> 00:09:16,910
which you may currently have in your school

176
00:09:16,910 --> 00:09:18,030
or your university.

177
00:09:18,030 --> 00:09:20,470
So they are designed for open bench work

178
00:09:20,470 --> 00:09:23,110
and they are not designed to handle pathogens.

179
00:09:23,110 --> 00:09:30,290
They are basically designed for basic teaching and research.

180
00:09:30,290 --> 00:09:35,290
BSL-2 are designed for usage by primary health services

181
00:09:35,650 --> 00:09:38,950
and they have diagnostic services as well and research.

182
00:09:38,950 --> 00:09:40,690
And in this case, you must have

183
00:09:40,690 --> 00:09:43,170
your good microbiological techniques in place

184
00:09:43,170 --> 00:09:46,810
in terms of SOPs as well as PPEs.

185
00:09:46,810 --> 00:09:49,570
And you must install appropriate signage

186
00:09:49,570 --> 00:09:51,270
to communicate by risk.

187
00:09:52,410 --> 00:09:57,170
In BSL-2, you can upgrade your level of safety

188
00:09:57,170 --> 00:10:03,020
by installing a biological safety cabinet.

189
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:07,100
BSL-3 are generally referred to as containment facilities

190
00:10:07,100 --> 00:10:09,740
and they are basically designed

191
00:10:09,740 --> 00:10:11,660
for specialist diagnostic services

192
00:10:11,660 --> 00:10:13,980
and research involving pathogens

193
00:10:13,980 --> 00:10:16,400
belonging to the higher risk groups.

194
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:20,060
In this case, PPEs are compulsory.

195
00:10:20,060 --> 00:10:23,720
Directional air flow is also compulsory.

196
00:10:24,620 --> 00:10:28,020
Access control for both biosecurity and biosafety

197
00:10:28,020 --> 00:10:32,140
and all laboratory procedures involving biological agents

198
00:10:32,140 --> 00:10:35,380
have to be carried out within a biological safety cabinet

199
00:10:35,380 --> 00:10:38,060
and not on an open bench.

200
00:10:38,060 --> 00:10:39,820
I will explain this to you

201
00:10:39,820 --> 00:10:44,940
when we get into the section on aerosols.

202
00:10:44,940 --> 00:10:48,060
BSL-4 are maximum containment laboratories.

203
00:10:48,060 --> 00:10:50,180
As I mentioned earlier,

204
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:52,980
these laboratories are very expensive to build,

205
00:10:52,980 --> 00:10:54,620
operate and maintain

206
00:10:54,620 --> 00:10:57,380
and they are generally located at the national level.

207
00:10:57,380 --> 00:11:00,500
You may only have one of these in your country.

208
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:02,780
They have aloe country, shower on exit.

209
00:11:02,780 --> 00:11:05,620
They also have pressurized suits.

210
00:11:05,620 --> 00:11:07,540
So everyone wears a pressurized suit

211
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:11,620
in which case the pressure is maintained

212
00:11:11,620 --> 00:11:14,380
at positive with reference to the laboratory.

213
00:11:14,380 --> 00:11:16,580
So in case your suit gets punctured,

214
00:11:16,580 --> 00:11:18,140
there's an outflow of air

215
00:11:18,140 --> 00:11:21,300
which protects you from any spills.

216
00:11:22,420 --> 00:11:26,740
And this use a class three biological safety cabinet

217
00:11:26,740 --> 00:11:35,550
which is hard ducted into the handling units.

218
00:11:35,550 --> 00:11:37,870
You may also come across this designation

219
00:11:37,870 --> 00:11:39,410
with a prefix A.

220
00:11:39,410 --> 00:11:43,250
So A refers to animal containment facilities.

221
00:11:43,250 --> 00:11:45,270
And these are generally used in research

222
00:11:45,270 --> 00:11:47,830
involving rodents,

223
00:11:47,830 --> 00:11:49,230
murine models,

224
00:11:49,230 --> 00:11:50,390
simian models,

225
00:11:50,390 --> 00:11:52,110
as well as other animal models.

226
00:11:52,110 --> 00:11:54,390
So we have until A, B, S, L, four.

227
00:11:55,870 --> 00:11:57,590
And this may be the case

228
00:11:57,630 --> 00:12:02,270
where some research facilities intend conducting research

229
00:12:02,270 --> 00:12:07,110
involving viruses using murine models or simian models.

230
00:12:07,110 --> 00:12:09,550
Plant containment facilities are designated

231
00:12:09,550 --> 00:12:11,830
with the prefix BSL1P

232
00:12:13,830 --> 00:12:15,710
and two BSL4P.

233
00:12:17,310 --> 00:12:20,870
And these are generally used for containment of plants

234
00:12:20,870 --> 00:12:25,870
because plants can serve as a potential source of pathogens,

235
00:12:25,950 --> 00:12:26,950
not to humans,

236
00:12:26,950 --> 00:12:31,430
but to other plants within the vicinity of the facility.

237
00:12:31,430 --> 00:12:34,790
These facilities may also be required in your country

238
00:12:34,790 --> 00:12:39,750
if you are working with genetically modified organisms,

239
00:12:39,750 --> 00:12:42,570
such as genetically modified plants.

240
00:12:42,570 --> 00:12:46,870
Now the designation plus at the end of this,

241
00:12:46,870 --> 00:12:48,230
for instance BSL3,

242
00:12:48,230 --> 00:12:52,430
indicates that the BSL containment facility

243
00:12:52,430 --> 00:12:54,510
has incorporated some design element

244
00:12:55,510 --> 00:12:56,710
from BSL4.

245
00:12:56,710 --> 00:13:04,020
So this is actually enhanced BSL3 plus.

246
00:13:04,020 --> 00:13:06,540
We now move on to facility design

247
00:13:06,540 --> 00:13:08,900
and I would like to draw your attention

248
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:19,730
to airflow and air pressure.

249
00:13:19,730 --> 00:13:20,890
Now in the forums,

250
00:13:20,890 --> 00:13:24,090
one of the participants has mentioned

251
00:13:24,090 --> 00:13:25,370
a very interesting point,

252
00:13:25,370 --> 00:13:28,570
which I've incorporated into my slides.

253
00:13:28,570 --> 00:13:31,330
They have asked about selection of the site

254
00:13:31,330 --> 00:13:34,550
in terms of the risk to the environment.

255
00:13:34,550 --> 00:13:38,450
Now BSL3 or a BSL4 facility

256
00:13:38,450 --> 00:13:43,450
is a very expensive facility to build and operate.

257
00:13:43,690 --> 00:13:47,270
So ideally it should be located in a site

258
00:13:47,270 --> 00:13:50,010
in which there is a low risk of natural disasters,

259
00:13:50,010 --> 00:13:52,250
such as earthquakes, flooding,

260
00:13:52,250 --> 00:13:55,550
or other tsunamis for instance.

261
00:13:55,550 --> 00:13:58,690
And it should be located in an area

262
00:13:58,690 --> 00:14:00,970
with a low population density,

263
00:14:00,970 --> 00:14:05,530
not in a centralized place such as a city.

264
00:14:05,530 --> 00:14:08,970
It should have access to electricity and water,

265
00:14:08,970 --> 00:14:11,450
as these are critical components.

266
00:14:11,450 --> 00:14:15,090
And in case of contingencies or emergencies,

267
00:14:16,330 --> 00:14:19,470
the facility should be accessible to the hazmat teams,

268
00:14:19,470 --> 00:14:21,610
which may involve personnel

269
00:14:21,610 --> 00:14:24,210
who has been specially trained

270
00:14:24,210 --> 00:14:29,210
to manage spills and other risks associated

271
00:14:30,090 --> 00:14:31,490
with biological agents.

272
00:14:31,490 --> 00:14:35,170
Generally each country has a hazmat team,

273
00:14:35,170 --> 00:14:37,290
and these hazmat teams are deployed

274
00:14:37,290 --> 00:14:40,130
in the event of a breach of containment.

275
00:14:40,130 --> 00:14:43,530
They consist of specialized personnel

276
00:14:43,530 --> 00:14:47,350
or specialists who are trained to manage chemical

277
00:14:47,350 --> 00:14:49,810
as well as biological spills.

278
00:14:49,810 --> 00:14:51,290
And as far as possible,

279
00:14:51,290 --> 00:14:54,290
please do not site your facility in a conflict zone

280
00:14:54,290 --> 00:14:58,410
in which there is a potential for war

281
00:14:59,330 --> 00:15:00,850
or terrorism.

282
00:15:00,850 --> 00:15:03,250
These are some of the issues

283
00:15:03,250 --> 00:15:08,900
which must be addressed when selecting a site.

284
00:15:08,900 --> 00:15:13,480
Now, one of the key factors or the key elements

285
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:16,200
which protects the laboratory user and the environment

286
00:15:16,200 --> 00:15:17,880
is directional air flow.

287
00:15:17,880 --> 00:15:21,200
And I will explain this to you in a diagram

288
00:15:22,720 --> 00:15:24,560
during the course of this lecture.

289
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:29,520
So air is basically fluid and it can be pressurized

290
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:32,000
and this makes it an indispensable agent

291
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,840
for the containment of biological agents.

292
00:15:34,840 --> 00:15:36,600
Generally facilities are designed

293
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:40,000
so that the air flows from the clean to the dirty zones

294
00:15:41,520 --> 00:15:43,840
prior to release into the environment

295
00:15:43,840 --> 00:15:47,720
via the HEPA filters.

296
00:15:47,720 --> 00:15:50,720
Let us look at this concept of clean and dirty.

297
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:54,040
Now this is your laboratory worker

298
00:15:54,040 --> 00:15:56,560
and this is the biological agent.

299
00:15:56,560 --> 00:15:59,680
Now, if there is a directional air flow

300
00:15:59,680 --> 00:16:04,160
moving from this side towards the laboratory worker,

301
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:05,760
this is not acceptable.

302
00:16:05,760 --> 00:16:09,920
As any aerosol generated by this biological agent

303
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:12,480
during operations such as pipetting and centrifugation

304
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:15,400
will be blown directly towards the laboratory worker.

305
00:16:15,400 --> 00:16:18,420
And this will pose a risk to the laboratory worker.

306
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:22,920
Directional air flow ensures that the air flows

307
00:16:22,920 --> 00:16:23,760
from this side.

308
00:16:23,760 --> 00:16:28,760
So you have your HEPA filtered air flowing via baffles

309
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:32,880
or you can imagine this like an air conditioning vent

310
00:16:32,880 --> 00:16:36,340
but only in the case of BSL two and three,

311
00:16:36,340 --> 00:16:39,200
the air is actually filtered

312
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:43,240
and this air generally flows over the laboratory worker

313
00:16:43,240 --> 00:16:46,080
and it exits from the other end of the facility

314
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:49,200
also via a HEPA filter.

315
00:16:49,200 --> 00:16:52,480
So this is the concept of directional air flow.

316
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:54,440
So that's the exit of the dirty air.

317
00:16:54,440 --> 00:16:56,680
So this area is generally designated as clean

318
00:16:56,720 --> 00:16:59,360
and this area is designated as dirty.

319
00:16:59,360 --> 00:17:01,000
So in the case of a spill, for instance,

320
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:04,280
you spill this biological agent on the floor over here,

321
00:17:05,640 --> 00:17:08,360
your cleanup should be done from this direction.

322
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:12,200
Do not step into this area and try to clean up the spill.

323
00:17:12,200 --> 00:17:14,400
You try and clean up from the other side.

324
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:19,640
I will discuss this in spill cleanups.

325
00:17:19,640 --> 00:17:22,320
Now, another important aspect about air

326
00:17:22,320 --> 00:17:27,320
is it has fluidity and we can pressurize it

327
00:17:28,320 --> 00:17:31,520
or you can generate negative pressure.

328
00:17:31,520 --> 00:17:33,880
So a containment facility is maintained

329
00:17:33,880 --> 00:17:35,520
at a negative pressure with reference

330
00:17:35,520 --> 00:17:37,240
to the external environment.

331
00:17:37,240 --> 00:17:40,160
This ensures that aerosols containing biological agents

332
00:17:40,160 --> 00:17:45,630
are contained within the negative pressure zone.

333
00:17:45,630 --> 00:17:48,030
Okay, now one of the procedures

334
00:17:48,030 --> 00:17:50,790
we generate aerosols is pipetting.

335
00:17:50,790 --> 00:17:52,830
Another one is centrifugation

336
00:17:52,830 --> 00:17:56,750
and these aerosols are generated within a facility

337
00:17:56,750 --> 00:17:58,390
and the purpose of the PPE

338
00:17:58,390 --> 00:17:59,950
or personal protective equipment

339
00:17:59,950 --> 00:18:01,910
is to protect the laboratory worker

340
00:18:01,910 --> 00:18:04,590
or the laboratory user from the aerosols.

341
00:18:04,590 --> 00:18:09,590
However, aerosols can be generated at a very high load

342
00:18:10,390 --> 00:18:14,310
and this is why we need to constantly circulate air

343
00:18:14,310 --> 00:18:15,590
through this facility.

344
00:18:15,590 --> 00:18:20,590
So the air circulation serves like a washing system.

345
00:18:20,950 --> 00:18:22,790
It washes through the facility.

346
00:18:22,790 --> 00:18:25,230
Imagine you have used a detergent on your clothes

347
00:18:25,230 --> 00:18:27,190
and you wash it multiple times

348
00:18:27,510 --> 00:18:29,470
until the detergent moves away.

349
00:18:29,470 --> 00:18:30,750
This is a laundry procedure

350
00:18:30,750 --> 00:18:34,390
but the same analogy applies in this facility.

351
00:18:34,390 --> 00:18:36,470
So there's a constant flow of air

352
00:18:36,470 --> 00:18:39,030
taking away the aerosols

353
00:18:39,030 --> 00:18:43,850
and filtering them out through the HEPA filter.

354
00:18:43,850 --> 00:18:48,310
Okay, HEPA filters are built to certain standards.

355
00:18:48,310 --> 00:18:51,250
So according to European standards,

356
00:18:51,250 --> 00:18:55,250
a HEPA air filter must remove at least 99.95%

357
00:18:55,970 --> 00:19:00,970
of particles whose diameter is equal to 0.3 micron.

358
00:19:02,410 --> 00:19:08,020
Okay, so this is what a HEPA filter is designed to do.

359
00:19:08,020 --> 00:19:09,940
Okay, now this is a HEPA filter

360
00:19:09,940 --> 00:19:12,300
which is mounted in a special housing

361
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:17,220
and each HEPA filter is fitted onto the facility itself

362
00:19:17,220 --> 00:19:18,300
via a seal.

363
00:19:18,300 --> 00:19:20,740
So air can only pass through this filter

364
00:19:20,740 --> 00:19:22,280
and not through the seal.

365
00:19:22,280 --> 00:19:24,380
And when HEPA filters are generally changed,

366
00:19:24,380 --> 00:19:26,260
they have to be bagged in and bagged out.

367
00:19:26,260 --> 00:19:28,820
So you fit a big bag on this HEPA filter

368
00:19:29,700 --> 00:19:32,260
and this has to be done by specialized personnel

369
00:19:32,260 --> 00:19:34,560
and the entire filter is bagged out

370
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:36,740
and then decontaminated

371
00:19:36,740 --> 00:19:38,980
and you cannot reuse a HEPA filter.

372
00:19:38,980 --> 00:19:40,540
It's generally disposed of.

373
00:19:40,540 --> 00:19:43,480
Now this all adds to the cost of the facility itself.

374
00:19:44,540 --> 00:19:48,540
Now HEPA filters work by trapping particles

375
00:19:48,540 --> 00:19:49,420
within a matrix.

376
00:19:49,420 --> 00:19:52,380
So they have a filter matrix and this is compressed.

377
00:19:52,380 --> 00:19:55,580
So particles are intercepted

378
00:19:55,580 --> 00:19:58,580
and if they contain viruses or bacteria

379
00:19:58,580 --> 00:20:01,220
in aerosolized form, they will be trapped.

380
00:20:01,220 --> 00:20:04,020
They impact these filters and they cannot pass through.

381
00:20:04,020 --> 00:20:07,060
So as a particle passes through,

382
00:20:07,060 --> 00:20:09,600
the velocity is generally reduced

383
00:20:09,600 --> 00:20:12,920
and this particle then gets trapped in this filter.

384
00:20:12,920 --> 00:20:15,060
Now you will ask me what happens to particles

385
00:20:15,060 --> 00:20:17,540
which are less than 0.3 micron

386
00:20:17,540 --> 00:20:22,220
and because of the multiple layers in a HEPA filter,

387
00:20:22,220 --> 00:20:24,780
the particles are actually trapped

388
00:20:24,780 --> 00:20:27,580
at any one of these levels

389
00:20:27,580 --> 00:20:31,140
and you have clean air coming out from this filter.

390
00:20:31,140 --> 00:20:34,100
Now generally as HEPA filters age,

391
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:35,960
they tend to become more efficient

392
00:20:35,960 --> 00:20:38,040
as more and more particles are trapped

393
00:20:38,040 --> 00:20:39,700
but this does not mean the HEPA filter

394
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:41,020
should not be changed.

395
00:20:41,020 --> 00:20:43,700
You must change it as the matrix may degrade

396
00:20:43,700 --> 00:20:51,340
over a period of time.

397
00:20:51,340 --> 00:20:53,500
We now move on to the facility design

398
00:20:53,500 --> 00:20:54,380
of the building itself.

399
00:20:54,380 --> 00:20:56,420
Now this is a cross section of the facility.

400
00:20:56,420 --> 00:20:57,900
It's a cross section view.

401
00:20:57,900 --> 00:21:00,340
So if you can imagine a facility,

402
00:21:00,340 --> 00:21:02,860
this one is the roof and this is the cross section.

403
00:21:02,860 --> 00:21:06,560
Now a facility generally has a minimum of three floors.

404
00:21:06,560 --> 00:21:08,380
So the upper floor is a service floor

405
00:21:08,380 --> 00:21:10,900
which handles all the air,

406
00:21:10,900 --> 00:21:14,460
your HEPA filters, compressors, pumps,

407
00:21:14,460 --> 00:21:16,040
air conditioning equipment.

408
00:21:16,040 --> 00:21:18,980
The lower floor or the floor which is in the basement

409
00:21:18,980 --> 00:21:23,200
handles the EDS or the effluent disposal system

410
00:21:23,200 --> 00:21:27,160
which traps all the liquid coming from the facility

411
00:21:27,160 --> 00:21:31,280
including the liquid which comes after your shower.

412
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:36,280
And here the liquid is treated using chemical reagents

413
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:39,640
and finally disposed off to the system

414
00:21:40,720 --> 00:21:43,180
which may involve your sewage system.

415
00:21:43,180 --> 00:21:47,080
You also have your administrative offices and laboratories.

416
00:21:47,080 --> 00:21:51,720
This is the design of the facility itself.

417
00:21:51,720 --> 00:21:54,680
Now for laboratory design,

418
00:21:54,700 --> 00:21:56,720
this is a cross sectional view.

419
00:21:56,720 --> 00:21:59,160
We have our change areas in which we change

420
00:21:59,160 --> 00:22:04,160
from our street clothes to our laboratory clothes

421
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:07,240
which may involve or include PPEs

422
00:22:07,240 --> 00:22:10,320
and we move to an airlock which is negative pressure

423
00:22:10,320 --> 00:22:16,410
and then we move on to the containment laboratory.

424
00:22:16,410 --> 00:22:18,630
Now the service floors which are located

425
00:22:18,630 --> 00:22:22,190
in the upper reaches of this facility

426
00:22:22,190 --> 00:22:24,570
consist of the HEPA filters.

427
00:22:24,570 --> 00:22:26,190
Now these HEPA filters are designed

428
00:22:26,230 --> 00:22:29,710
so as to pump in clean air which protects the personnel

429
00:22:30,590 --> 00:22:34,230
and then the dirty air or the contaminated air

430
00:22:34,230 --> 00:22:36,270
is pumped out via HEPA filters

431
00:22:36,270 --> 00:22:38,730
before it is released into the environment.

432
00:22:39,670 --> 00:22:43,750
Generally, there is something known as a strobic fan here

433
00:22:43,750 --> 00:22:48,510
and this strobic fan resembles a jet engine, a turbine.

434
00:22:48,510 --> 00:22:51,510
So these strobic fans are installed

435
00:22:51,510 --> 00:22:52,670
on the roof of the facility.

436
00:22:52,670 --> 00:22:55,150
They will pump the filtered air high

437
00:22:55,210 --> 00:22:56,670
into the environment.

438
00:22:56,670 --> 00:22:58,270
So we'll have a plume which may go up

439
00:22:58,270 --> 00:23:02,670
to 100 to 200 meters high and this dilutes the air

440
00:23:02,670 --> 00:23:06,190
and reduces the likelihood of exposure

441
00:23:06,190 --> 00:23:09,950
to the biological agent to the environment

442
00:23:09,950 --> 00:23:13,860
and the general public.

443
00:23:13,860 --> 00:23:16,660
Now I have prepared a special video for you

444
00:23:17,540 --> 00:23:20,380
in which I have walked through the service floors

445
00:23:20,380 --> 00:23:23,580
and you can access this from your lecture notes.

446
00:23:23,580 --> 00:23:26,400
I have inserted a link in your PDF lecture notes.

447
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:33,560
You can click on the link and view this video.

448
00:23:33,560 --> 00:23:36,560
This is what I have depicted in the video.

449
00:23:36,560 --> 00:23:39,000
Now these are the HEPA filters

450
00:23:39,000 --> 00:23:41,640
which are basically letting the air go out.

451
00:23:41,640 --> 00:23:42,920
So everything which exists,

452
00:23:42,920 --> 00:23:45,400
the facility passes through this HEPA filter.

453
00:23:45,400 --> 00:23:47,540
There are multiple filters inside this

454
00:23:47,540 --> 00:23:49,600
and as you can see, this is what is known

455
00:23:49,600 --> 00:23:53,960
as a Magna-Helic gauge, which is a air pressure gauge

456
00:23:53,960 --> 00:23:58,120
and these are critical to monitoring of the system.

457
00:23:58,120 --> 00:23:59,680
This is another HEPA filter.

458
00:23:59,760 --> 00:24:03,120
This is the one in which air enters into the system

459
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:05,280
and this HEPA filter is located directly

460
00:24:05,280 --> 00:24:07,120
on top of the laboratory.

461
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:10,320
And these are the strobic fans.

462
00:24:10,320 --> 00:24:14,280
So the air from this particular HEPA filter

463
00:24:14,280 --> 00:24:16,440
exits through this strobic fan.

464
00:24:16,440 --> 00:24:17,920
It is pumped.

465
00:24:17,920 --> 00:24:19,680
There's a turbine and it's pumped high

466
00:24:19,680 --> 00:24:24,440
into the atmosphere in a stream and it's diluted.

467
00:24:24,440 --> 00:24:27,560
So in the case of a contingency or an emergency

468
00:24:27,560 --> 00:24:30,720
which involves compromising of this filter,

469
00:24:30,720 --> 00:24:33,040
for example, there's a breakdown in this filter,

470
00:24:33,040 --> 00:24:37,080
the strobic fan will still push or dilute the air

471
00:24:38,240 --> 00:24:40,460
before it is released into the environment.

472
00:24:40,460 --> 00:24:44,200
Generally, facilities are designed so as to shut down

473
00:24:44,200 --> 00:24:46,340
in the event of a breach of containment,

474
00:24:46,340 --> 00:24:48,280
which means that everything shuts down

475
00:24:48,280 --> 00:24:54,340
so nothing is released into the environment.

476
00:24:54,340 --> 00:24:56,500
Now with regard to effluent disposal,

477
00:24:56,500 --> 00:24:59,000
effluent refers to all the waste water

478
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:00,840
coming from your facility.

479
00:25:00,860 --> 00:25:04,000
This will include the waste water after your shower,

480
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,860
after hand washing, as well as from autoclaves.

481
00:25:06,860 --> 00:25:10,000
All the waste water is directed to the basement

482
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,200
in which there is a holding tank.

483
00:25:12,200 --> 00:25:15,520
This holding tank is designed to hold water

484
00:25:15,520 --> 00:25:19,120
for at least a week's capacity of this laboratory.

485
00:25:19,120 --> 00:25:21,560
So in my particular laboratory,

486
00:25:21,560 --> 00:25:25,600
our holding tank capacity is around 5,000 liters of water.

487
00:25:26,840 --> 00:25:28,360
From the holding tank, it is pumped

488
00:25:28,360 --> 00:25:30,520
into the disinfection tank

489
00:25:30,540 --> 00:25:35,540
in which a decontaminate or a sterilizing agent is added.

490
00:25:36,600 --> 00:25:37,840
This may be bleached.

491
00:25:37,840 --> 00:25:39,980
However, the SOP needs to be developed

492
00:25:39,980 --> 00:25:43,840
for each biological agent.

493
00:25:43,840 --> 00:25:48,640
After disinfection for the duration of the contact time,

494
00:25:50,060 --> 00:25:55,060
the disinfected fluid is pumped into the neutralization tank

495
00:25:56,520 --> 00:25:59,620
where the disinfectant is neutralized prior to release.

496
00:25:59,620 --> 00:26:02,620
Now all these procedures must be monitored

497
00:26:02,620 --> 00:26:05,300
and records must be maintained at all times

498
00:26:05,300 --> 00:26:18,690
as part of the process of continuous quality improvement.

499
00:26:18,870 --> 00:26:20,530
We now move on to biosecurity

500
00:26:20,530 --> 00:26:25,210
and facilities must be designed for biosecurity.

501
00:26:25,210 --> 00:26:28,290
And this must be implemented or incorporated

502
00:26:28,290 --> 00:26:30,450
into the building design.

503
00:26:30,450 --> 00:26:32,890
And this may include physical security

504
00:26:32,890 --> 00:26:34,650
in the form of excess controls

505
00:26:34,650 --> 00:26:37,810
as well as information security

506
00:26:37,810 --> 00:26:43,590
or data security.

507
00:26:43,590 --> 00:26:46,150
I will now move on to biological safety cabinet

508
00:26:46,150 --> 00:26:49,910
or what we call the BSC, biological safety cabinet.

509
00:26:49,910 --> 00:26:53,230
I have discussed in an earlier lecture

510
00:26:54,670 --> 00:26:57,110
and the biological safety cabinet is designed

511
00:26:57,110 --> 00:27:02,630
to protect the laboratory user from aerosols.

512
00:27:02,630 --> 00:27:05,510
These are processes which generate aerosols.

513
00:27:05,510 --> 00:27:09,430
For instance, a simple pipetting procedure

514
00:27:09,430 --> 00:27:10,630
may generate an aerosol.

515
00:27:10,630 --> 00:27:13,710
Pipetting onto a slide can generate an aerosol

516
00:27:14,510 --> 00:27:18,070
and centrifugation can also generate aerosols.

517
00:27:18,070 --> 00:27:22,630
Now this kind of centrifuge must preferably not be used

518
00:27:22,630 --> 00:27:27,310
in biological safety level three and four facility

519
00:27:28,830 --> 00:27:31,910
as the rotor is a potential source

520
00:27:31,910 --> 00:27:35,330
of the biological agent when you open it.

521
00:27:35,330 --> 00:27:39,790
Now what happens in centrifuge is that your liquid

522
00:27:39,790 --> 00:27:44,270
or your fluids are spun at very high RPMs,

523
00:27:44,270 --> 00:27:47,470
14,000 to maybe 20,000 RPM.

524
00:27:47,470 --> 00:27:51,270
And this procedure generates aerosols within this cavity

525
00:27:51,270 --> 00:27:53,110
or this rotor cavity.

526
00:27:54,190 --> 00:27:58,110
As a general rule in biological safety level two,

527
00:27:58,110 --> 00:27:59,710
three and four laboratories,

528
00:27:59,710 --> 00:28:01,710
the rotor unit should be sealed.

529
00:28:01,710 --> 00:28:05,430
So you seal this rotor and you move the entire rotor

530
00:28:05,430 --> 00:28:09,110
into your biological safety cabinet before you open it.

531
00:28:09,130 --> 00:28:10,870
You do not open the rotor outside

532
00:28:10,870 --> 00:28:13,130
of the biological safety cabinet,

533
00:28:13,130 --> 00:28:15,190
which brings us to another point.

534
00:28:15,190 --> 00:28:17,550
Centrifuges cannot be operated

535
00:28:17,550 --> 00:28:20,150
within a biological safety cabinet

536
00:28:20,150 --> 00:28:27,360
as they generate their own egg patterns.

537
00:28:27,360 --> 00:28:30,680
This is the general layout of a biological safety cabinet.

538
00:28:30,680 --> 00:28:32,280
This is the cross section.

539
00:28:32,280 --> 00:28:34,800
Now the room air enters into this section

540
00:28:34,800 --> 00:28:37,320
and then it circulates here through this plenum.

541
00:28:37,320 --> 00:28:41,040
There'll be a motorized fan here

542
00:28:41,040 --> 00:28:45,040
and it's HEPA filtered out of the facility.

543
00:28:45,040 --> 00:28:47,680
So now this section may be hard ducted,

544
00:28:47,680 --> 00:28:48,900
which means that this is fitted

545
00:28:48,900 --> 00:28:51,840
into the air handling unit itself

546
00:28:51,840 --> 00:28:55,680
and the air is pumped out from this unit.

547
00:28:55,680 --> 00:28:57,060
Oh, it may be thimble ducted,

548
00:28:57,060 --> 00:29:00,700
which means that output of this section

549
00:29:00,700 --> 00:29:03,920
is actually not connected into the HEPA filter

550
00:29:03,920 --> 00:29:05,440
or the hard ducted system.

551
00:29:05,440 --> 00:29:06,760
It is actually thimble ducted,

552
00:29:06,760 --> 00:29:09,360
which means there is another hood on top of this

553
00:29:09,360 --> 00:29:10,920
which draws in the air.

554
00:29:11,640 --> 00:29:14,920
Now, this is an example of a thimble ducted facility.

555
00:29:14,920 --> 00:29:19,720
A thimble ducted system allows the air to be balanced

556
00:29:19,720 --> 00:29:22,160
because if it is hard ducted into the system

557
00:29:22,160 --> 00:29:23,720
and the HEPA filter fails,

558
00:29:23,720 --> 00:29:26,680
the pressure of air being drawn from this

559
00:29:26,680 --> 00:29:32,060
can damage the BSC in itself.

560
00:29:32,060 --> 00:29:35,700
Okay, this is the class 2B1 BSC

561
00:29:35,700 --> 00:29:39,380
in which 70% of the air is exhausted

562
00:29:39,380 --> 00:29:42,720
and 30% of the air is recirculated.

563
00:29:42,720 --> 00:29:47,200
The HEPA filter traps 99.97% of particles

564
00:29:47,200 --> 00:29:53,950
of 0.3 micron in diameter and below.

565
00:29:53,950 --> 00:29:55,790
This is an example of the ducting.

566
00:29:55,790 --> 00:29:57,390
Now, if you look at the top

567
00:29:57,390 --> 00:30:00,270
of this particular biological safety cabinet

568
00:30:00,270 --> 00:30:01,790
in my laboratory,

569
00:30:01,790 --> 00:30:04,830
this is an example of a hard ducted cabinet.

570
00:30:04,830 --> 00:30:08,350
Now, this EBC pipe over here

571
00:30:08,350 --> 00:30:10,910
connects directly to the HEPA filter.

572
00:30:10,910 --> 00:30:15,070
The air is pumped out from this BSC

573
00:30:15,070 --> 00:30:17,690
and connected to the centrifugal blower

574
00:30:17,690 --> 00:30:19,750
to the HEPA filter here.

575
00:30:19,750 --> 00:30:21,190
And this, as you can see,

576
00:30:21,190 --> 00:30:24,790
is what is known as a controller

577
00:30:24,790 --> 00:30:27,010
which consists of a vane.

578
00:30:27,010 --> 00:30:30,770
It's actually like a disk which sits here.

579
00:30:30,770 --> 00:30:32,690
And based on the pressure,

580
00:30:32,690 --> 00:30:35,510
the servo motor will open or close the vane.

581
00:30:36,470 --> 00:30:37,310
Okay, there's a vane here.

582
00:30:37,310 --> 00:30:38,150
So it looks like this.

583
00:30:38,150 --> 00:30:39,750
So if you look through this pipe,

584
00:30:39,750 --> 00:30:42,670
you will see this is the pipe itself.

585
00:30:42,670 --> 00:30:44,810
And this is the vane.

586
00:30:44,830 --> 00:30:46,490
And this is connected to the motor.

587
00:30:46,490 --> 00:30:49,590
So now this vane or this particular unit

588
00:30:49,590 --> 00:30:52,770
is rotated based on the airspeed.

589
00:30:52,770 --> 00:30:55,930
And all of this is controlled by a computer.

590
00:30:55,930 --> 00:31:00,090
Now, this links back to the centralized computers.

591
00:31:00,090 --> 00:31:03,390
In the event of a failure of this particular unit,

592
00:31:03,390 --> 00:31:04,530
this unit will take over

593
00:31:04,530 --> 00:31:08,490
and still direct the air flow out of the facility.

594
00:31:08,490 --> 00:31:13,530
That's the purpose of this hard ducting.

595
00:31:13,530 --> 00:31:15,150
This is the general layout.

596
00:31:15,150 --> 00:31:18,910
So you work from this side to the side

597
00:31:18,910 --> 00:31:22,670
based on whether you are left-handed or right-handed.

598
00:31:22,670 --> 00:31:24,230
And this is your double back

599
00:31:24,230 --> 00:31:26,470
in which you dispose of your vase.

600
00:31:26,470 --> 00:31:28,390
This is your operational area.

601
00:31:28,390 --> 00:31:30,550
And this may be a pipette stand.

602
00:31:30,550 --> 00:31:32,990
You try and not block this air.

603
00:31:32,990 --> 00:31:34,710
It's not advisable to block the air

604
00:31:34,710 --> 00:31:38,350
from this particular vents.

605
00:31:38,350 --> 00:31:42,190
This air is flowing and maintaining

606
00:31:42,430 --> 00:31:45,810
a safe working environment within the facility.

607
00:31:45,810 --> 00:31:48,610
So this is an example of this grill here.

608
00:31:48,610 --> 00:31:51,590
Do not place any kind of tissues or tubes

609
00:31:51,590 --> 00:31:53,430
or anything on top of this

610
00:31:53,430 --> 00:31:55,430
as the air flow will be compromised.

611
00:31:55,430 --> 00:31:58,970
And this is the upper section here.

612
00:31:58,970 --> 00:32:00,470
And this is where the air flow is done.

613
00:32:00,470 --> 00:32:04,510
So imagine this situation where your work here,

614
00:32:04,510 --> 00:32:05,790
you work within this facility

615
00:32:05,790 --> 00:32:08,090
and the air is constantly directed down,

616
00:32:08,090 --> 00:32:09,470
but it's not blown towards you.

617
00:32:09,470 --> 00:32:11,590
It's actually drawn away from you.

618
00:32:11,590 --> 00:32:16,460
So you work in a safe zone.

619
00:32:16,460 --> 00:32:19,100
This is the class 2B1 biological safety cabinet,

620
00:32:19,100 --> 00:32:20,740
which is far more complex.

621
00:32:20,740 --> 00:32:22,980
So the clean air is exhausted out

622
00:32:22,980 --> 00:32:26,180
and the air is circulated back using a HEPA filter

623
00:32:26,180 --> 00:32:28,460
onto the biological agent.

624
00:32:28,460 --> 00:32:30,860
And this protects the laboratory user.

625
00:32:31,780 --> 00:32:35,220
So this is the biological safety cabinet

626
00:32:35,220 --> 00:32:42,010
which I've depicted in the photographs.

627
00:32:42,010 --> 00:32:45,230
Now, when we work with biological safety cabinets,

628
00:32:45,230 --> 00:32:47,250
these are the points which you should note.

629
00:32:47,270 --> 00:32:48,570
First is certification.

630
00:32:48,570 --> 00:32:51,850
Biological safety cabinets will have to be certified

631
00:32:51,850 --> 00:32:54,970
in accordance with your national regulations.

632
00:32:54,970 --> 00:32:58,490
And this involves testing of this system

633
00:32:58,490 --> 00:33:01,530
by a certified service provider.

634
00:33:03,010 --> 00:33:05,250
So generally, biological safety cabinets

635
00:33:05,250 --> 00:33:08,110
are certified for a duration of 12 months.

636
00:33:09,410 --> 00:33:11,850
The location of the cabinet,

637
00:33:11,850 --> 00:33:15,010
the cabinet cannot be located near doors

638
00:33:15,030 --> 00:33:18,370
or areas which generate strong occurrence.

639
00:33:18,370 --> 00:33:20,770
Materials must be placed in the facility

640
00:33:20,770 --> 00:33:24,690
so as to ensure that no air flow is blocked.

641
00:33:25,850 --> 00:33:30,850
The facility must be operated with an SOP.

642
00:33:33,250 --> 00:33:36,490
There are specific SOPs for cleaning and sterilization

643
00:33:37,410 --> 00:33:42,410
and disinfection and maintenance must be done periodically

644
00:33:43,090 --> 00:33:47,010
as well as you must have an SOP for emergency maintenance.

645
00:33:47,010 --> 00:33:48,970
So these are some of the criteria

646
00:33:48,970 --> 00:33:50,570
which must be taken into account

647
00:33:50,570 --> 00:33:55,600
when working with a biological safety cabinet.

648
00:33:55,600 --> 00:33:57,340
So the placement should be in areas

649
00:33:57,340 --> 00:33:59,280
which are free from air currents

650
00:33:59,280 --> 00:34:01,820
and other biological safety cabinets.

651
00:34:01,820 --> 00:34:03,760
Ergonomics should be taken into account

652
00:34:03,760 --> 00:34:06,560
as working in a biological safety cabinet

653
00:34:06,560 --> 00:34:08,940
for an extended duration of time

654
00:34:08,940 --> 00:34:10,340
can be very challenging

655
00:34:10,340 --> 00:34:14,100
and can lead to repetitive strain injury.

656
00:34:14,100 --> 00:34:16,860
And the ducting, which is what I mentioned here,

657
00:34:16,860 --> 00:34:20,220
the hard ducting of the biological safety cabinet

658
00:34:20,220 --> 00:34:22,660
must be designed so as to ensure

659
00:34:22,660 --> 00:34:25,380
that the air flow is uniform

660
00:34:25,380 --> 00:34:29,340
and there is no backflow of air.

661
00:34:29,340 --> 00:34:30,480
This is what I meant.

662
00:34:30,480 --> 00:34:33,620
So with reference to the placement,

663
00:34:33,620 --> 00:34:35,700
I assume that this is the flow plan of your lab.

664
00:34:35,700 --> 00:34:37,620
You enter through this door

665
00:34:37,620 --> 00:34:40,380
and this is the direction of airflow.

666
00:34:40,380 --> 00:34:42,700
Your biological safety cabinet should be located

667
00:34:42,700 --> 00:34:45,160
at the far end of this facility

668
00:34:45,160 --> 00:34:47,540
and this will be your laboratory workbench.

669
00:34:47,540 --> 00:34:51,180
Now you may not open the sample over here.

670
00:34:51,180 --> 00:34:53,340
So when you receive your samples,

671
00:34:53,340 --> 00:34:55,660
you transfer them, you open the box,

672
00:34:55,660 --> 00:34:58,180
but you transfer the vials containing the sample

673
00:34:58,180 --> 00:35:00,820
directly into the biological safety cabinet

674
00:35:00,820 --> 00:35:01,980
and then you discard them

675
00:35:01,980 --> 00:35:05,980
so you can have a waste disposal unit here

676
00:35:05,980 --> 00:35:09,900
and following on to your autoclave.

677
00:35:09,980 --> 00:35:11,460
You have a double door to clear.

678
00:35:11,460 --> 00:35:15,680
So this is the ideal situation for the laboratory setup.

679
00:35:15,680 --> 00:35:18,300
So this autoclave should be operated

680
00:35:18,300 --> 00:35:20,540
only when there are no personnel in the room.

681
00:35:20,540 --> 00:35:22,140
And the autoclave generate heat

682
00:35:22,140 --> 00:35:24,340
and this can disturb the occurrence as well.

683
00:35:24,340 --> 00:35:28,200
So generally in most facilities, autoclaves are automated.

684
00:35:28,200 --> 00:35:30,860
So you complete your work, you bag it,

685
00:35:30,860 --> 00:35:33,400
you place it in the autoclave,

686
00:35:33,400 --> 00:35:35,060
you seal the autoclave, you start it

687
00:35:35,060 --> 00:35:37,060
and you exit the facility.

688
00:35:37,060 --> 00:35:39,420
And generally you have a double door.

689
00:35:39,420 --> 00:35:42,300
So when the autoclaving procedure is completed,

690
00:35:42,300 --> 00:35:45,440
the material can be withdrawn from this facility

691
00:35:45,440 --> 00:35:51,000
and sent for incineration.

692
00:35:51,000 --> 00:35:53,480
So BSCs must be certified periodically

693
00:35:53,480 --> 00:35:55,000
as I mentioned earlier

694
00:35:55,000 --> 00:35:57,360
in accordance with your national standards.

695
00:35:57,360 --> 00:36:00,800
If national standards do not exist in your country,

696
00:36:00,800 --> 00:36:02,560
please refer to a reference standard

697
00:36:02,560 --> 00:36:04,360
from another jurisdiction

698
00:36:04,360 --> 00:36:07,840
and adapt it for your countries

699
00:36:07,840 --> 00:36:13,660
or your laboratories requirements.

700
00:36:13,660 --> 00:36:16,500
So this is a standard operating procedure.

701
00:36:16,500 --> 00:36:20,500
You will learn how to write a standard operating procedures

702
00:36:20,500 --> 00:36:23,180
or a single standard operating procedure

703
00:36:23,180 --> 00:36:29,060
during the lecture on administrative controls.

704
00:36:29,060 --> 00:36:31,480
The first thing which you need to check

705
00:36:31,480 --> 00:36:34,320
before you operate a biological safety cabinet

706
00:36:34,320 --> 00:36:36,920
is whether it been certified.

707
00:36:36,920 --> 00:36:39,740
You then decontaminate prior to use

708
00:36:39,740 --> 00:36:42,060
using the ultraviolet light,

709
00:36:42,060 --> 00:36:46,580
you surface wipe using a recommended disinfectant,

710
00:36:46,580 --> 00:36:48,420
you place your instruments.

711
00:36:48,420 --> 00:36:49,940
In case of a spill cleanup,

712
00:36:49,940 --> 00:36:53,220
you must have an appropriate SOP to clean up spills.

713
00:36:54,260 --> 00:36:57,180
You must have an appropriate SOP for waste disposal

714
00:36:57,180 --> 00:36:59,860
and post usage decontamination.

715
00:36:59,860 --> 00:37:03,220
I will discuss the development of an SOP

716
00:37:03,220 --> 00:37:05,960
in the lecture on administrative controls.

717
00:37:05,960 --> 00:37:07,500
And we will look specifically

718
00:37:07,500 --> 00:37:10,820
at the biological safety cabinet in the SOP

719
00:37:10,820 --> 00:37:16,470
as your lecture will lead from one module to another.

720
00:37:16,470 --> 00:37:19,870
Now I have prepared a special tutorial for you all.

721
00:37:20,970 --> 00:37:24,090
You can access it by clicking on this link

722
00:37:24,090 --> 00:37:26,610
in your lecture notes in the PDF file.

723
00:37:26,610 --> 00:37:29,330
And this is a tutorial on working

724
00:37:29,330 --> 00:37:35,220
in a biological safety cabinet.

725
00:37:35,220 --> 00:37:37,340
I prepared another specific tutorial

726
00:37:37,340 --> 00:37:39,880
on a procedure known as double bagging.

727
00:37:39,880 --> 00:37:43,900
And you may access it on this link,

728
00:37:43,900 --> 00:37:45,840
click on this link in your lecture notes.

729
00:37:45,860 --> 00:37:49,120
And this is a very short tutorial on double bagging.

730
00:37:49,120 --> 00:37:51,360
So generally in a biological safety cabinet,

731
00:37:51,360 --> 00:37:56,120
we have our waste container and we have one bag,

732
00:37:56,120 --> 00:38:00,780
which is a biosafety bag, which is a polypropylene bag, PPE.

733
00:38:00,780 --> 00:38:02,060
And we have another bag.

734
00:38:02,060 --> 00:38:04,800
Now you will ask me, why do you need two bags?

735
00:38:04,800 --> 00:38:06,520
One placed inside the other.

736
00:38:06,520 --> 00:38:10,680
So the reason is when you place your pipette tips

737
00:38:10,680 --> 00:38:12,960
and your other waste material here,

738
00:38:12,960 --> 00:38:15,440
there is a likelihood of this bag being punctured

739
00:38:15,440 --> 00:38:16,960
and compromised.

740
00:38:16,960 --> 00:38:20,360
So in this case, you may have a spill in the lab.

741
00:38:20,360 --> 00:38:24,200
So generally what we do in a biological safety cabinet,

742
00:38:24,200 --> 00:38:30,860
we shift this entire waste container into the cabinet.

743
00:38:30,860 --> 00:38:33,980
We dispose our waste into this.

744
00:38:33,980 --> 00:38:38,980
And after completion, we seal it using adhesive tape

745
00:38:39,580 --> 00:38:42,260
or using ties as the case may be.

746
00:38:42,260 --> 00:38:45,220
And then we decontaminate the surface using,

747
00:38:45,240 --> 00:38:47,380
for instance, 70% alcohol.

748
00:38:48,660 --> 00:38:52,140
And following this, we discard this in the autoclave.

749
00:38:52,140 --> 00:38:55,300
This ensures that there is no spill.

750
00:38:55,300 --> 00:38:58,160
So double bagging is a very important procedure

751
00:38:58,160 --> 00:39:04,540
which you must adopt in your laboratory.

752
00:39:04,540 --> 00:39:07,180
This brings us to the end of this module.

753
00:39:07,180 --> 00:39:09,940
I hope you have understood the concepts.

754
